@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+The existing homepage now includes a rewards registration form. It submits natively
+to `POST /api/rewards/signup`, so the browser can populate the hidden
+`autocomplete="email-verification-token"` field at submission time. JavaScript is
+optional. No OTP, magic link, or other verification email is sent. Missing or
+invalid browser proof blocks account creation with an actionable message.
+
+## Build and runtime
+
+Requires Node.js 24+ and pnpm. Install with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
+`pnpm build` runs syntax checks and isolated cryptographic/storage tests, then
+packages the app in `dist/`. The build does not import the server entrypoint,
+start any server, use a browser, or contact live email providers.
+
+When ready to run the application, `pnpm start` serves the homepage and endpoint
+on `PORT` (default 11222). A static host cannot execute verification or create
+accounts. The old static-only start command has been replaced with this Node
+server. Existing navigation destinations other than Rewards are retained.
+
+Set runtime environment variables using `.env.example` as a reference (the
+server does not automatically load that file):
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, with scheme and optional port.
+  Required in production. Audience and request-origin checks use this trusted
+  setting, not forwarded headers. Local default: `http://localhost:11222`.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL`: a valid Email Verification origin-trial
+  token registered for that origin. The server injects it into the homepage.
+  There is deliberately no invented token or claim of universal browser support.
+- `DATA_DIR`: persistent writable directory for SQLite (default `./data`).
+  Restrict access and include it in your backup process.
+- `NODE_ENV=production` and `PORT` as appropriate for the host.
+
+The API is experimental and requires a participating browser, a participating
+email provider, an active provider session in the same browser profile, and any
+required user permission. Origin-trial registration and token renewal are
+deployment prerequisites. See the current official documentation:
+https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier
+
+## Verification and storage
+
+Each page gets a random 10-minute nonce bound to an HttpOnly, SameSite session
+cookie (Secure and `__Host-` on HTTPS). Verification checks DNS delegation from
+`_email-verification.<email-domain>`, HTTPS issuer metadata, provider public keys,
+issuer signature, browser key-binding signature, email ownership claims, exact
+origin, session nonce, `sd_hash`, and token times. Discovery connections are
+pinned to public IPs and use bounded responses, deadlines, and no redirects.
+Public key sets with or without `kid` are supported. SD-JWT parsing and binding
+validation use `@sd-jwt/core`; signature verification uses `jose`.
+
+Only verified requests insert a rewards account. The SQLite transaction consumes
+the challenge and inserts the account atomically. Email uniqueness is case
+insensitive; duplicates never overwrite an account. Stored fields include name,
+email, a salted scrypt password hash, issuer, and verification/creation times.
+Raw tokens and passwords are not persisted or logged. No email service exists in
+this app. There is no OTP fallback.
+
+Basic per-process request limits are included. Run one instance with its persistent
+SQLite volume; use shared storage and shared rate limits before scaling to multiple
+instances. Configure appropriate limits at your reverse proxy, too. This change
+covers registration; sign-in, ordering, and rewards redemption are outside its scope.
+
+Verification performed for this change is limited to build checks and isolated
+tests. No browser verification, dev server, preview server, or server entrypoint
+execution was used.
